WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:01.510
Hello there, I'm Gav.

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:02.970
Welcome to this Slow Mo Guys video.

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.880
This one is sponsored by Mio.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.770
It's a water enhancer that
when you squirt into a nice cup

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.400
of water, you get very visually
appealing, colorful flume,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.220
reminiscent of our ink in the water video

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.570
from a few years ago.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.490
Today, we'll be taking
inspiration from that

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.930
and recreating a classic
science experiment.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.650
Replacing the glass of
water with this massive tank

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.000
and dropping molten thermite into it,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.360
recording it obviously

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.270
at over a thousand frames a second in 4K.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:34.210
The next thing I'm gonna do
is get into some safety gear

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.060
and tell you not to try this at home.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.843
I don't really know how you would though.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.430
This here is our lovely pot of thermite.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.260
Whenever I ask the question,
how bright is thermite,

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.690
the answer seems to be, yes.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:50.180
So what we're gonna do is a small test.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.200
I'm gonna learn a lot
about how bright it is,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.620
what I should be exposing for.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.960
Currently, I'm at F8
on a 90 degree shutter,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.520
at 1,000 frames a second.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.563
So let's see how bright this truly is.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.690
Okay, as you can see, thermite
is indeed very bright,

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.920
completely blown out at an F8.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.700
So for this next shot, I
exposed F16 to the point

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.760
where it looks like this
was filmed at night,

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.120
even though it's a bright
sunny day and only now,

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.053
am I seeing any information
in those molten blobs.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.440
What we're using here is
the most common combination

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:44.780
of materials to make thermite,

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.720
and that is very finely
ground up aluminium

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.203
and iron oxide, which is
pretty much just rust powder.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.180
We've learned a lot of
good things from the test.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.920
I'm happy with my exposure, ISO 320,

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.240
90 degree shutter, F 16,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.940
unheard of F stop for me doing this.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.550
The thermite will melt through
this pot, go into the water,

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.910
the sand will hopefully
stop it from melting

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:30.910
through the bottom of the tank.

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:34.140
This is a plastic tank
that is slightly leaking,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.303
so not sure how many gos
we'll get from this tank.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.630
This footage is actually
slightly different

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:09.520
to footage of if you're just

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.330
pouring molten metal into water.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:12.163
If you've ever seen that,

00:03:12.163 --> 00:03:15.130
you'll notice that as the
molten liquid descends,

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:16.790
it cools down, it becomes more red

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:18.650
and eventually sort of just stacks up

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:19.970
as solid metal on the bottom.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.990
The thermite, the reaction
continues all the way down.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.540
It remains molten, even
after it lands on the sand.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.070
The reaction here isn't
actually using any oxygen

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.850
from the surrounding air
and it's not creating CO2

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:33.030
as a byproduct.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.810
What we're doing, is
transferring the oxygen

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.940
from the iron oxide to the aluminium,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:40.380
and that is a massive exothermic reaction.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.510
The two reaction products
that we're left with,

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:46.230
which is just plain old
iron and aluminium oxide,

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:50.713
end up over 2000 degrees
celsius, hence the brightness.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.850
Slightly different effect
when it lands in water,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.710
there's a lot of extra
bubbles and gunk coming out

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:03.610
along with the thermite.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.700
So in order to see a
little bit more of that,

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.550
I'm just gonna expose for the sky.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.770
So this will be very bright thermite,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.013
but you'll be able to
see everything around it.

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.640
So we know that this reaction
is just a direct transfer

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.830
between the two materials involved,

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:53.030
and it's not creating
any gases as a byproduct.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.610
So the bubbles are actually just a result

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:58.470
of the insane temperatures
immediately flashing

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.390
the surrounding water to steam,

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:01.900
and especially in this shot on the right,

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.540
you can see the insane
amount of steam bubbles

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.823
being produced by the heat
of the molten thermite.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:23.480
The water itself doesn't
actually quench the reaction,

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:25.180
so you kind of just have to wait it out.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:26.350
And at the end, you're just left

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:29.000
with very brittle pieces of iron.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.330
Well, I think we've got all the shots

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.290
that I wanted to see.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:32.500
Some lovely looking footage.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.970
However, slight problem,
we do have 10 pounds

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.020
of thermite left over that I
don't really want to take home,

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:40.300
so we just put it in a tin can,

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.910
and we're just gonna get
rid of it all at once.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.190
For reference, everything
we've done so far

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.040
has been no bigger than three pounds.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.500
You need quite a lot of heat to start

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.340
this thermite reaction, the
little thing you're seeing

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.670
sticking out the side of the can there,

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:04.023
is a small magnesium strip.

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:18.400
It's honestly a lot going on here.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.470
You can see that the thermite
reaction has started,

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.760
but a lot of the powder
has fallen under the water

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:25.603
and is now exploding
back out like a volcano.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:30.540
Interestingly, if I wind it back,

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:35.143
look at how quickly the side
of this can becomes molten.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.030
So the reaction has just started
once all these sparks clear

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:43.570
out of the way, just around
where it's being held in place,

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:44.650
it's already glowing.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:49.200
So in real time, that is
around a third of a second

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.630
that this can became liquid basically,

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:54.883
causing it just to give up and fall in.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:06.860
Well, I think we've ended
up with some wonderful,

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.280
artsy looking footage there,
very satisfying to look at.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.840
I could watch that for ages.

